— i.e. the two shipped example configs must parse. (`projectRoot` walks up from `#filePath` to the `.git` dir; `testUtil.swift:7`.)

**Docs are enforced by two other mechanisms instead:**

1. **Compile-time.** `help: <name>_help_generated` doesn't exist unless the `.adoc` exists and `generate.sh` has been run. Missing doc = missing symbol = build failure.

2. **CI drift check.** `/Users/bogac/dev/forgejo/AeroSpace/test.sh` (verbatim):

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
cd "$(dirname "$0")"
source ./script/setup.sh

./build-debug.sh -Xswiftc -warnings-as-errors
./swift-test.sh

./.debug/aerospace -h > /dev/null
./.debug/aerospace --help > /dev/null
./.debug/aerospace -v | grep -q "0.0.0-SNAPSHOT SNAPSHOT"
./.debug/aerospace --version | grep -q "0.0.0-SNAPSHOT SNAPSHOT"

./lint.sh
./generate.sh
./script/check-uncommitted-files.sh

echo
echo "✅ All tests have passed successfully"
```

`check-uncommitted-files.sh` exits 1 on any `git status --porcelain` output. So: edit an `.adoc`, forget to re-run `generate.sh` and commit the result → CI red.

Also note `lint.sh` runs **periphery** with `--strict`, which flags unused declarations. This is why every impl has `/*conforms*/` markers and why some declarations carry `// periphery:ignore`. And it forbids `Task { }` — use `Task.startUnstructured`.

